00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
how are you doing today sir  i'm good sir how are you doing i have a  cat screaming outside my door hopefully  you don't hear her  [Laughter]  why is she so angry your cat what's  going on i think she's just desperate to  be part of this interview i mean she's a  big fan of your work  i can't blame her listen i i can't blame  her  stop bogarting me let the cat in man  so let me start by saying i saw your  film yesterday and i really enjoyed it  and i you have a knack of writing  dialogue that feels very realistic  and i'm just curious how do you manage  to sort of capture realism like that and  put it on the page  um well first of all i have to equally  credit my co-writers tama bitigan and  noe debre because they  they were very much a part of all that  dialogue and all that work with me but  thank you for that  um you know i think some of it is just  research right and sort of finding and  by that i don't just mean like  book research but spending time with  people spending time with people in this  world be it  uh bill from oklahoma be it roughnecks  uh  be it uh people in marseille uh and just  sort of immersing myself in the culture  as a writer it's one of my favorite  things to do just show up kind of drop  in start to make connections and with  this one some of those connections were  built up over  years right and and really just sort of  listening to the rhythms and the sounds  and uh the tone of the people i'm  writing  uh and then letting that infuse over  time so um  you know it's something i love doing i  love trying to find it i love trying to  be as efficient as possible with it  as minimal as possible that when i can  um  but you know it's really one of the  great joys when those characters start  to come alive and talk you know it's  just  it's one of the thrills of the job quite  frankly  here's my curveball for you uh if you  get the financing for any project  tomorrow  what would you make and why oh man  look i felt that way about stillwater  right like i wrote this script  and um you know i pitched it first to my  friend steve golan who's now passed away  steve's one of the producers on the  movie in this very hotel that i'm giving  this  interview from right now we had lunch  and down there and i said i got this  movie about a roughneck who goes to  marseille to get his daughter out of  prison  it's going to be somewhat like a  redemption story but it's going to have  more dimension he's like i'm in  and uh that's how life should be right  like and somehow that remained true we  found partners at participant and david  lindy he read the script he's like i'm  in  now you've seen the movie it's a  complicated movie right it defies  genre and expectation i think in ways  it's going to be exciting  and challenging for audiences but like  the fact that these people stepped up in  such a way and said go make your movie  go to marseille make your movie you want  to make like i feel so fortunate to do  that so  i got to start with stillwater i still  sometimes pinch myself  that i was able to go make that movie  and make it with matt damon and  i mean that wholeheartedly like when i  watch the movie it can  you know 100 miles from where we shot it  i'm like man i can't believe we got to  do this  especially now after a year and a half  of being locked up in my house  i'm like man the fact that i got to go  live in france and make  exactly the story i wanted to we all  felt lucky  matt me the crew the guys who i've  worked with in the past  we all felt like man you don't get a  chance to do things like this very often  in your career  so i you know yeah i could guess what  that other film could be but i got to  acknowledge  i think the blessings i had to go make  this movie it was just it was uh  i felt fortunate i liked the way how you  shot this film  and in oklahoma it was like static shots  anamorphic lenses you go to marseille  and it's  you know more handheld you had a very  specific aesthetic choices uh where you  were filming the movie can you sort of  talk about why you wanted  uh this camera work and the way you shot  it yeah it was just sort of  you know feeling that a little bit more  you know uh  space around bill with the anamorphic  specifically  in oklahoma and feeling him a little bit  more rooted to the ground right a little  more  of the earth which is what bill is as  opposed to when we go to marseille and  we start feeling the sea  and the sky and the light of marseille  and um you know we don't spend a lot of  time in oklahoma and we really two  things had to happen one  we really needed to feel the differences  and the uh  of the space and the energy the visual  energy of those spaces need to be really  clearly defined  two which is a little off point is matt  damon's performance right like  matt had to come we only spend four  minutes in oklahoma before we drop him  into marseille  he needed to be there fully formed fully  dimensional for us to really appreciate  this man's journey  but to go back to your visual like that  was the idea once we got off that plane  in marseille  and we're sort of mysteriously following  this roughneck who seems to know exactly  where he's going in this very specific  city  um we wanted to not only feel the  mystery of that but the energy of that  uh and that's when we started going  handheld and and steady for almost the  entire movie  until we returned to oklahoma um that  was very much  uh by design it's something my dp and i  massa  nobutakinagi talked a lot about  i'm always obsessed with learning about  the editing because that's the final  rewrite and ultimately it's where the  movie comes together it's make or break  uh can you talk about the challenges you  encountered in the editing room  did you ever have a radically different  version or you know a different version  of certain storylines  no what we had was a lot more movie on  this one we shot a lot of movie  we i needed to because i knew i wasn't  going to go back to marseille with matt  it was going to be too hard to organize  that and  and we just had a lot of scripts so it  was really finding that and you know i  credit my  my editor tom mcardle he's edited all my  films i think of him as a  fourth writer on this project and um you  know he just has  a real way of distilling distilling and  distilling and getting to the essence of  the movie and pushing on me  to push on my own script and my own  notions of the picture  i think what was really interesting  about this one was that like because we  shut down you know we're almost done  with the movie  the march going into the shutdown of  covid and we let it go we didn't pick it  up again until august tom came out we  edited in my basement for a while which  was super cool  and uh and then we shut it down again  and we picked it up  this past february uh really february  march probably to finish the movie and  complete it for can  but we had these breaks of like four or  five months and it was  super interesting for a movie this  complex with this many  threads and this many lanes to be able  to stand back and look at the movie  fresh and be like  okay what what what is essential here  and what is not  and where are we losing our audience and  where are we you know where are we doing  it right  um having that space from the movie and  then being able to look at fresh was  incredibly helpful did you ever have  like a three hour  cut that you were like super happy with  305 i think  really um but you know by the way the  studio never was like you know get your  cut down get your cut down they were  just like  you know we were doing it to ourselves  in fact we went out to show  we went out to show the studio that  caught last march right before the  shutdown  they were super happy with the movie and  it was about 20 minutes longer  now they knew we weren't done working on  it but tom and i came back like he's we  had to watch it three times with each  with participant with focus and one  other time and we got back  and make making ourselves watch it fresh  with people we're like all right we know  what we got to do we took 20 minutes out  of the movie like that  and it wasn't dropping any one  particular scene you know so  um it you know you're right to focus on  editing it's just such a you know  obviously critical but just  to me it's always fascinating how like  you think you're done and then you  reapproach something  there's one montage sequence about three  quarters of the way through the movie  um that shows sort of matt lang deeper  into marseille  and we did not get that right we did not  get that sequence right until about  two weeks from finishing editing after  about a year and a half  total and it just took time we just  couldn't see it before that  and thank goodness we had the time even  though it was not for the right reasons  necessarily  i have to stop there i'm just going to  say congrats on the film i hope it's a  big hit for you guys  thank you thank you thank you for your  time

